This is the same decision idea written as a recursive binary search. A speed is valid when the sum of the rounded-up pile times is at most `h`. Valid speeds let the search continue on the left, while invalid speeds require a faster rate.

```java
class Solution {
    public int minimumRateToEatBananas(int[] nums, int h) {
        int largestPile = 0;
        for (int pile : nums) {
            largestPile = Math.max(largestPile, pile);
        }
        return search(nums, h, 1, largestPile);
    }

    private int search(int[] nums, int h, int left, int right) {
        if (left == right) {
            return left;
        }

        int speed = left + (right - left) / 2;
        if (canFinish(nums, h, speed)) {
            return search(nums, h, left, speed);
        }
        return search(nums, h, speed + 1, right);
    }

    private boolean canFinish(int[] nums, int h, int speed) {
        long hours = 0;
        for (int pile : nums) {
            hours += (pile + (long) speed - 1) / speed;
            if (hours > h) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

The base case is when both bounds point to one speed. For a single pile, the answer can be `1` when there are enough hours, and it can also be the pile size when only one hour is available.

Time complexity: `O(n log M)`.
Space complexity: `O(log M)` because of the recursive calls.

The array is only read and is not changed.

The hours needed go down whenever the eating speed goes up. That means the speeds look like invalid values followed by valid values. I search for the first valid speed with binary search.

The helper returns early if the hour count already passes `h`, which avoids checking the rest of the piles in that case.

```java
class Solution {
    public int minimumRateToEatBananas(int[] nums, int h) {
        int right = 0;
        for (int pile : nums) {
            right = Math.max(right, pile);
        }

        int left = 1;
        while (left < right) {
            int speed = left + (right - left) / 2;
            if (canFinish(nums, h, speed)) {
                right = speed;
            } else {
                left = speed + 1;
            }
        }

        return left;
    }

    private boolean canFinish(int[] nums, int h, int speed) {
        long hours = 0;
        for (int pile : nums) {
            hours += (pile + (long) speed - 1) / speed;
            if (hours > h) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

For example, with `nums = [3, 6, 7, 11]` and `h = 8`, speed `6` needs `6` hours, so the right side moves left. Speed `3` needs `10` hours, so the left side moves right. The first speed that works is `4`.

Time complexity: `O(n log M)`, where `M` is the largest pile.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

A `long` is used for the total hours so that adding many pile times does not overflow.

A small improvement is to start the lower bound near the average bananas per hour. The answer cannot be below `total bananas / h` when that value is rounded up, because Koko must eat all bananas in the available hours. The bound is still kept at least `1`.

The binary search then checks whether each candidate speed can finish all piles.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int minimumRateToEatBananas(vector<int> nums, int h) {
        long long totalBananas = 0;
        int largestPile = 0;

        for (int pile : nums) {
            totalBananas += pile;
            largestPile = max(largestPile, pile);
        }

        int left = max(1LL, (totalBananas + h - 1) / h);
        int right = largestPile;

        while (left < right) {
            int speed = left + (right - left) / 2;
            long long hours = 0;

            for (int pile : nums) {
                hours += (pile + (long long) speed - 1) / speed;
                if (hours > h) {
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (hours <= h) {
                right = speed;
            } else {
                left = speed + 1;
            }
        }

        return left;
    }
};
```

For `nums = [5, 5]` and `h = 3`, the total is `10`, so the starting lower bound is `ceil(10 / 3) = 4`. This does not always equal the answer, but it is a safe starting point.

An empty input is not expected by the platform. With normal positive piles, the upper bound is always at least the lower bound.

Time complexity: `O(n log M)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The input vector stays unchanged, and `long long` protects both the total bananas and the hour count.

I kept the search very direct here. Try each possible speed from 1 upward, calculate the hours for every pile, and stop at the first speed that fits in `h` hours. It is easy to follow, but it is much slower than binary search when the largest pile is very large.

For a pile of `pile` bananas and speed `speed`, the needed hours are `(pile + speed - 1) / speed`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int minimumRateToEatBananas(vector<int> nums, int h) {
        int largestPile = 0;
        for (int pile : nums) {
            largestPile = max(largestPile, pile);
        }

        for (int speed = 1; speed <= largestPile; speed++) {
            long long hours = 0;
            for (int pile : nums) {
                hours += (pile + speed - 1) / speed;
                if (hours > h) {
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (hours <= h) {
                return speed;
            }
        }

        return largestPile;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n * M)`, where `M` is the largest pile.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

This does not change the input vector. The main trade-off is the linear scan over all possible speeds.

